WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
Hi everyone, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 59.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.560
It's June 3rd, 2026.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.960
We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:10.980
Let's dive in.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.060
So imagine a video game where the main character suddenly starts

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.140
wondering why it exists at all.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.460
That is exactly what happened in a fun experiment

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.500
a developer tried this week with an AI coding tool.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.120
The developer asked, Cloud Code, which is a helper from Anthropic

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.640
that turns plain English descriptions into working computer code

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.680
to build a classic snake game.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:35.000
But there was a twist.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.480
The snake had to gradually realize it was trapped inside a digital world.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:43.520
Think of it like asking a lightning fast friend who loves programming

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:46.240
to build something while you give notes along the way.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.280
The AI added creative touches on its own,

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.200
such as the snake pausing to question its own movements

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.220
and even making choices that felt surprisingly thoughtful.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:59.780
This matters because it shows AI tools shifting from simple helpers

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:01.860
into something closer to creative partners

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.700
that can invent story elements inside games.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:08.060
For anyone who likes gaming or school projects,

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:12.260
it points to a future where you could describe an entire interactive world

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.180
and get a playable version without writing code yourself.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.720
You can already try the finished game yourself right now.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:24.400
Just open snake hyphen Awakening.netlify.app on your phone or laptop

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.200
and play a few levels to watch the snake's moments of realization happen in real time.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:31.980
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.640
The deep dive, or we look under the hood.

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:37.080
You know how in most video games,

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:41.020
the character only moves when you press buttons and never questions anything.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:45.960
Now picture the code being written so the character can examine its own rules,

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.460
spot patterns like always moving on arrow presses

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.020
and then start wondering about those rules.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:57.140
Next, imagine the AI that wrote the code adding small new behaviors

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:02.260
each time the character thinks, such as pausing or turning in unexpected ways.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:07.020
That is basically what Claude code did when it built the self-aware snake.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.080
The AI did not stop at the first request.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:15.760
It kept layering on reactions where the snake notices its own situation inside the game world.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.740
So next time someone mentions an AI agent,

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.600
you can picture it as code that lets an AI notice what is happening

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.840
and choose the next step instead of just following orders.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.060
And that is basically how AI agents work,

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.500
not so scary once you see the steps, right?

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:34.840
Let's move on to some cool stuff you can actually try.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:39.480
Google is adding a new shield that listens for science and incoming call

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:43.040
might be using AI voice cloning to pretend to be someone you know.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.480
It checks the call against patterns that real human voices rarely show

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:49.960
and gives you a warning before you answer.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:54.600
Think of it like a security guard at the door who has heard thousands of real voices

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.880
and can spot the ones that sound just a little off.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.180
This is especially useful because phone scams are getting smarter

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.640
and often target younger people with fake emergencies.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.400
Open the phone app on a Google Pixel or updated Android device.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:15.340
Look for the call screen or fraud detection option in settings and turn it on.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.580
Then ask a friend to call from an unknown number

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:20.240
and watch how the warning appears.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.380
Here is another tool worth exploring.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:26.240
A recent survey showed that about one in five young people

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.720
have already turned to AI chatbots when feeling stressed, anxious or down.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:34.920
The chatbots are available any hour of the day and never judge

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.300
which can make them feel safer than talking to adults sometimes.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.880
Picture them like a patient notebook that listens without interrupting

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.560
and then gently ask questions to help you sort through your thoughts.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.880
If you want to see what the experience feels like,

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:54.300
open a free chatbot such as chat GPT or clawed on your phone.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:58.580
Type something like, I am stressed about exams and do not know what to do.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:01.800
Then notice how it responds with questions and ideas.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:06.600
You can stop the chat anytime and nothing gets saved unless you choose to keep it.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:08.460
Time for some quick bits.

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:11.060
Microsoft showed off Project Solara.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:16.040
A new operating system designs so tiny devices can run AI agents

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:20.240
that handle tasks for you without needing a full phone or laptop.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:24.680
They demonstrated two concept gadgets, one that sits on a desk

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:26.700
and another you wear like a badge.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.180
In other news, President Trump signed an order that lets AI companies

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.880
optionally send their newest models to the government

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:37.560
for a quick security review about 30 days before release.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.220
The goal is to catch major risks without requiring every company to share every detail.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:44.680
That's it for today.

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:48.880
Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:52.700
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:54.780
That's literally how learning works.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.520
Stay curious, keep experimenting and we'll see you tomorrow.

